Origami Diagram Maker - Licence
===============================

Licence version 1.0, in force from the first public release of the Software

Copyright (c) 2026 Thomas Krak. All rights reserved.

In short (this summary is for convenience; the terms below are what count):
- Free for individuals, including selling diagrams you make with it, as long as your income
  from them is at most EUR 1,000 per calendar year.
- Free for non-profit origami societies and non-profit schools and universities.
- A separate licence is needed above that income, for diagramming on commission for others,
  and for companies, publishers and other organisations (after a 30-day evaluation): just get
  in touch.
- You own the diagrams and project files you make. What you made under this licence stays
  yours to use and sell, also after you go over the income limit or your licence ends.
- You may share the unmodified installer, free of charge.
- No warranty.

By installing or using the Software, You accept this licence. If You do not accept it, do not
install or use the Software.


1. Definitions

"Software": the program Origami Diagram Maker as made available by the Author, in the form in
which it is distributed (including any source code files that it needs to run), together with
its updates, documentation and example files, and copies of all of these. Third-party
components included in it are dealt with in section 7.

"Author": Thomas Krak.

"You": the natural person or the Organisation that installs or uses the Software. An
Organisation is responsible for the use of the Software by the people acting on its behalf.

"Individual": a natural person using the Software for themselves: for private purposes or for
their own independent activities, including a business that they run in their own name (for
example as a sole trader or freelancer). A person who uses the Software on behalf of someone
else, for example as an employee, volunteer, board member or representative of an
Organisation, does not act as an Individual in that use.

"Organisation": any company, partnership, publisher, association, foundation, school,
university, government body or other organisation or legal entity. A business that an
Individual runs in their own name, without a separate legal entity, is not an Organisation.

"Non-profit Organisation": an origami society or association, or a school, university or
other educational institution, that is not run for profit: it does not distribute profits to
its members, owners, directors or others, and it uses any surplus only for its non-profit
purposes. Public schools and universities qualify; educational institutions and course
providers that are run for profit do not.

"Output": diagrams, images, PDF files and other material produced with the Software, and
project files saved by it.

"Commissioned Work": Output that You make at the request of another person or an
Organisation, for their purposes, in exchange for payment or other compensation, as explained
in section 2.3.

"Output Income": the income that You receive from Output and from works, products and
activities that contain or use Output, such as sales of diagrams or books, paid downloads,
royalties and advances, licence fees, and income from memberships, subscriptions, classes and
events, as far as it is attributable to Output. It is calculated as set out in section 2.4.

"Income Limit": EUR 1,000 of Output Income in a calendar year.


2. Free licences

2.1 The Author grants You a free, non-exclusive, non-transferable, worldwide licence to install
    and use the Software, subject to these terms:
    a) as an Individual, under the conditions of section 2.2;
    b) as a Non-profit Organisation, for the activities described in section 2.6; and
    c) for evaluation, as described in section 2.7.
    You may install the Software on as many devices as You need for the use that is covered,
    including shared computers. Each person who uses the Software must be covered: when acting
    for themselves, by their own licence; when acting on behalf of an Organisation, by the
    licence of that Organisation. Any other use requires a separate licence (section 3).

2.2 Individuals. As an Individual You may use the Software for any purpose, including
    commercial purposes such as selling diagrams, offering them for paid download or through
    paid memberships, and teaching, as long as:
    a) Your Output Income has not exceeded the Income Limit in the current calendar year, or
       You are still within the transition period of section 2.5; and
    b) You do not use the Software to make Commissioned Work.
    Whether You act as an Individual depends on whom You act for, not on whose computer You
    use or when You use it. Using the Software for Your employer, or otherwise on behalf of an
    Organisation, is not use as an Individual.

2.3 Commissioned Work. Output is Commissioned Work if You make it at the request of another
    person or an Organisation, for their purposes, and You receive payment or other
    compensation for making it or for its use; for example, diagramming a model for a
    designer, publisher or company for a fee. In this licence:
    a) payment or other compensation includes fees, honoraria, a share of revenue, and goods
       or services given in return;
    b) it does not include reimbursement of costs that You actually incur, a free copy of a
       publication that contains Your Output, free admission to an event at which Your Output
       is used or presented, or a credit or acknowledgement;
    c) Output that You make for someone without such compensation, for example for a friend,
       a colleague or a community organisation, is not Commissioned Work;
    d) Output for a work of Your own, such as Your own designs or a book of which You are the
       author or a co-author, is not Commissioned Work just because a publisher, shop or
       platform publishes or sells it and pays You for it, even if the publisher asked You to
       write it. Nor is Output that You make for an activity that You carry out Yourself, such
       as handouts for a class that You teach. The income from these counts as Output Income;
    e) Output that You make for a client as part of a larger paid assignment is Commissioned
       Work, even if the diagrams are only a small part of it;
    f) Output that You make for a Non-profit Organisation, for activities covered by section
       2.6, is not Commissioned Work, even if it pays You for it. What it pays You counts as
       Output Income.

2.4 Output Income is calculated per calendar year, as follows:
    a) Amounts count in the calendar year in which You receive them, whenever the Output was
       made or the sale took place. Royalties, advances and other delayed payments therefore
       count in the year in which You receive them.
    b) Count the price paid for Your sales and services without VAT or similar sales taxes,
       but before deduction of Your costs, such as materials, printing, shipping, and the
       commissions and fees that a platform, shop or payment provider keeps. Where someone
       else sells a work and pays You a share, such as a publisher paying royalties, count
       what is paid to You.
    c) Deduct refunds and chargebacks in the year in which You pay them back.
    d) Convert amounts in other currencies into euros at the rate that Your bank or payment
       provider applied or, failing that, at a published rate for the day on which You
       received them, such as the European Central Bank reference rate.
    e) Count goods or services that You receive instead of money at their usual price.
    f) If Output is only part of a work, product or activity, count only the part of its
       income that is attributable to the Output, using a reasonable method that You apply
       consistently. For example:
       - for a book, magazine or other publication: Your income from it, multiplied by the
         share of its models (or pages) that consists of Output;
       - for a class, membership, subscription, event or convention: the part of the price
         that is for the Output, such as the price that You charge, or would charge, for that
         Output on its own.
    g) If You share the income from a work with co-authors or other contributors, count only
       Your own share.

2.5 Going over the Income Limit. If Your Output Income exceeds the Income Limit in a calendar
    year:
    a) Your use of the Software up to that moment remains covered by this licence, and no fee
       or royalty is due for it;
    b) You may continue to use the Software under this licence for 60 days from the day
       on which the Income Limit was exceeded (the transition period). If You ask the Author
       for a separate licence within that period, the transition period lasts at least until
       30 days after the Author has answered Your request;
    c) after the transition period, You need a separate licence to use the Software for the
       rest of that calendar year. From 1 January of the next year, section 2.2 applies again;
    d) Output that You made while Your use of the Software was covered by this licence
       remains Yours (section 4), and You may continue to publish, sell, license and otherwise
       use it without a separate licence and without payment to the Author, also while Your
       Output Income exceeds the Income Limit and after this licence has ended.
    Going over the Income Limit is not a breach of this licence. Using the Software after the
    transition period without the required separate licence is.

2.6 Non-profit Organisations. A Non-profit Organisation may use the Software for its own
    activities that serve its non-profit purposes, such as convention books, magazines,
    newsletters, exhibitions, classes, workshops, teaching and research. This includes
    activities for which it charges fees or sells publications or merchandise, as long as the
    income is used only for its non-profit purposes. This licence covers the people who act on
    its behalf, such as its employees, volunteers, board members and teachers, and its pupils
    and students when they use the Software for their education there. It does not cover:
    a) making Output for others in exchange for payment or other compensation;
    b) use of the Software by another Organisation that does not qualify, even if it is
       related to the Non-profit Organisation, such as a trading subsidiary; or
    c) its members' own activities, for which they need a licence of their own, such as the
       free licence for Individuals.
    Contractors that it pays to make Output use the Software under their own licence; for
    Individuals, see section 2.3(f).

2.7 Evaluation. Anyone whose use is not covered by section 2.2 or 2.6, such as a company, a
    publisher or an Individual who wants to make Commissioned Work, may use the Software for
    evaluation only, during one evaluation period of 30 days:
    a) The evaluation period starts on the first day on which the Software is used by You, or
       on Your behalf, for a use not covered by section 2.2 or 2.6. There is one evaluation
       period per Individual and one per Organisation, however many people, devices,
       installations or versions are involved. Reinstalling the Software, moving to another
       computer, or installing an update or a new version does not start a new period. The
       Author may agree in writing to a new period, for example for a new major version.
    b) Evaluation means finding out whether the Software meets Your needs: testing its
       features, making test projects and sample diagrams, assessing them internally, and
       preparing work that You intend to continue under a separate licence.
    c) Output made during the evaluation period remains Yours (section 4) and You may keep
       it. You may not publish it, sell it, deliver it to a client or otherwise use it
       commercially, unless You obtain a separate licence that covers it.
    d) After the evaluation period, any further use requires a separate licence. An
       Individual who goes over the Income Limit gets the transition period of section 2.5,
       not an evaluation period.

2.8 Credit (a request, not a condition). If You publish, sell or otherwise make Output available
    to the public, the Author asks You to include a visible credit such as "Diagrams made with
    Origami Diagram Maker" in or alongside it. Not doing so does not affect Your licence.


3. Other use

Any use of the Software not covered by section 2 requires a separate written licence from the
Author. This includes use by Organisations other than Non-profit Organisations after the
evaluation period, use by Individuals after the transition period of section 2.5, and making
Commissioned Work. To ask for a licence, contact origamidiagrammaker@gmail.com. The Author
decides on such licences case by case and aims for simple terms that fit the nature and scale
of the use. Other non-profit organisations, such as libraries, museums and youth
organisations, are welcome to ask for a free licence. A separate licence may also cover use
that has already taken place. Where the terms of a separate licence differ from this licence,
the separate licence applies to the use that it covers.


4. Your Output

4.1 As between You and the Author, the Output is Yours. The Author claims no ownership of or
    other rights in Your Output, and no royalties or other payment for its use. You may also
    use the elements that the Software generates in Your Output, such as arrows, symbols and
    line styles, freely as part of that Output. Apart from sections 2.7(c) and 4.4, this
    licence places no conditions on what You do with Your Output, also after You go over the
    Income Limit or after this licence ends.

4.2 Whether Output is protected by copyright or other rights depends on the law and on the
    Output itself; this licence does not create such rights. Who owns Output that You make for
    someone else, such as Your employer or a client, is a matter between You and them.

4.3 This licence gives You no rights in material of others that Your Output contains or is
    based on, such as origami designs, crease patterns, diagrams, artwork or fonts made by
    others. The Software may draw on crease patterns, designs or other material that belong to
    others. You are responsible for having the rights to the designs that You diagram and for
    obtaining any permission that is needed.

4.4 The example files that come with the Software (crease patterns, projects and diagrams)
    are part of the Software. You may use them to learn, to try out the Software and as a
    starting point for Your own diagrams. Publishing or selling the example files themselves,
    or the example diagrams, requires the Author's permission, except as part of a copy of the
    Software that You distribute under section 5.


